<p>A technical SEO audit service diagnoses why search engines struggle to crawl, index, or rank your site — the infrastructure problems that no amount of good content fixes on its own. This is the paid, done-for-you version. Our <a href="/technical-website-audit/">technical audit guide</a> covers the same ground if you want to run the checks yourself.</p>

<h2 id="whats-included">What's Included</h2>

<p>Every technical SEO audit service engagement covers four areas end to end.</p>

<p><strong>Crawlability and indexation</strong> — full site crawl, robots.txt review, XML sitemap accuracy, redirect chains, canonical tag audit, and a Google Search Console indexation check against your actual page count.</p>

<p><strong>Site architecture</strong> — internal linking structure, URL hierarchy, orphaned pages, and crawl depth to your most important content.</p>

<p><strong>Speed and Core Web Vitals</strong> — LCP, INP, and CLS measured across your key templates, render-blocking resources, and mobile performance specifically. Google evaluates mobile speed separately from desktop.</p>

<p><strong>Structured data</strong> — schema markup validation across every page type, checked for errors and missed opportunities like FAQ, Product, or Article schema.</p>

<h2 id="service-vs-self-serve">Service vs Self-Serve</h2>

<p>Self-serve tools like Screaming Frog or Ahrefs Site Audit surface the same raw data a technical audit uses. The service adds three things a tool alone doesn't: a senior analyst separating real problems from tool noise, root-cause diagnosis when three different issues combine to cause one symptom, and a fix list ordered by actual impact rather than alphabetical or by severity label.</p>

<blockquote>A tool tells you 200 pages return a 4xx status. A technical SEO audit service tells you which 12 of those pages matter, why they broke, and which one to fix first.</blockquote>

<h2 id="common-findings">Common Findings</h2>

<p>Five issues recur most often across engagements. Crawl budget wasted on parameter-heavy URLs that should be blocked or canonicalised. Orphaned pages with no internal links pointing to them. Redirect chains three or four hops long instead of a single 301. Mobile Core Web Vitals failing while desktop passes. Sitemap entries that 404 or point to noindexed pages.</p>

